        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          Expedition Twelve Commander William McArthur and Flight Engineer Valery Tokarev were launched on the Soyuz TMA-7 spacecraft on September 30, 2005, from the Baikonur Cosmodrome in Kazakhstan for a two-day flight to link up to the Pirs Docking Compartment on the International Space Station (ISS). This was McArthur's fourth flight into space, and Tokarev flew to the space station in 1999 on a Shuttle mission. 
<P>
They were joined on the Soyuz by American businessman Gregory Olsen who spent eight days on the station under a contract signed with the Russian Federal Space Agency (Roscosmos) to become the third private citizen to reach the complex. Olsen returned to Earth on the Soyuz TMA-6 capsule with the Expedition Eleven crew on October 10, 2005. 
<P>
The six-month-plus stay of Expedition Twelve focused on station assembly preparations, maintenance, and science in microgravity. McArthur and Tokarev spent more than a week with the Expedition Eleven crew. Handover included briefings on station safety, systems, procedures, equipment and science. Station operations and maintenance took up a considerable share of Expedition Twelve's time, but science-oriented education activities, Earth observation, and scientific experiments, both with and without crew involvement, continued. 
<P>
Two to three spacewalks were planned during Expedition Twelve by McArthur and Tokarev. They focused on continued outfitting of station hardware and electrical systems and preparing external hardware for the addition of station elements. McArthur has two previous spacewalks, while Tokarev made his first.
<P>
Expedition Twelve saw the arrival of an unpiloted Russian Progress cargo vehicle filled with food, fuel, water, and supplies. The crewmembers also relocated their Soyuz spacecraft from the Pirs docking port to the Zarya docking port to free the Pirs airlock for spacewalks.
<P>
Many experiments from earlier Expeditions remain on board the space station and will continue to benefit from the long-term research platform provided by the orbiting laboratory. These experiments include: 
<P>
<UL>
<LI><B>Behavioral Issues Associated with Isolation and Confinement: Review and Analysis of Astronaut Journals</B> obtains information on behavioral and human factors related to the design of the equipment and procedures. It also monitors sustained human performance during long-duration missions.</LI>
<LI><B>Foot/Ground Reaction Forces During Space Flight (Foot)</B> studies the load on the lower body and muscle activity in crewmembers working on the station. This study will provide better understanding of lower-extremity bone and muscle loss experienced by astronauts in microgravity. The results of this experiment will help in planning future space flights and offers the potential to help better understand, prevent and treat osteoporosis - a disease occurring mostly in women where the bones become fragile, break easily and heal slowly - on Earth.</LI>
<LI><B>Renal Stone</B> collects urine samples from the crew and tests a possible countermeasure for preventing kidney stone formation.</LI>
<LI><B>Space Flight-Induced Reactivation of Latent Epstein-Barr Virus (Epstein-Barr)</B> performs tests to study changes in human immune function using blood and urine samples collected before and after space flight. The study will provide insight for possible countermeasures to prevent the potential development of infectious illness in crewmembers during flight.</LI>
</UL>
<P>
McArthur and Tokarev will return to Earth in April 2006 aboard the Soyuz TMA-7 spacecraft.
